  "body": "### Summary\nAs the PO running the five-tester trial, I want the service to refuse a short access code and to tell me at startup whether access is restricted, so that a typo or an empty variable cannot open the trial to everyone. The access code is the only thing that restricts the trial: the Funnel address is a public `https` name.\n\nRefs #635 (finding 2 of the UX-11 review).\n\n### What happens today\n- `UX_CODE_ACCES` is read without any check (`tooling/ux-survey/internal/contenu/config.go:42`). Measured on loopback at `ffa9418d`: the service starts with a three-character code and accepts it. The \"20 random characters at least\" rule exists only in the README, the spec §6 and `deploy/ux-survey.env.exemple`.\n- With an empty value the access check is simply not installed (`internal/web/middleware.go:156-159`) and the startup line (`cmd/ux-survey/main.go`, \"démarrage\") does not say so: a trial deployed with the variable missing looks the same in the log as a restricted one.\n\n### Scope\n- `ChargerConfig` fails when `UX_CODE_ACCES` is set and shorter than 20 characters (message without the value).\n- The startup log line carries `acces_restreint: true|false`, never the code.\n- README and spec §10 say the service refuses a shorter code.\n- Optional, same change: the PO checklist of `docs/ux/questionnaire/verification-image.md` (point 8) asks to read that line after `up`.\n\n### Acceptance criteria\n- A config test: a 19-character code is refused, a 20-character one accepted, an empty one accepted (seen to fail before the change).\n- A test or the CI image job reads `acces_restreint` in the startup line, and no log line holds the code.\n- `go vet ./... && go test -race ./...` green.",
